course: everyday
lesson: 276
title: Money in town and staying safe in crowds
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: When you carry money in town, keep it where it is hard to lose and hard to grab: a zipped pocket, a wallet or a purse that closes, not a loose back pocket or an open hand.
explain: A cash machine (an ATM in the USA, a cashpoint in the UK) asks for a secret number called a PIN. Use it with an adult, and cover the keypad with your other hand, because people nearby or a hidden camera could watch the PIN.
explain: Big crowds at festivals, matches, parades and fireworks are exciting, but it is very easy to get separated. Before you go in, agree a meeting point with your adult, and wear a wristband with their phone number so staff can call them.
explain: Example: a boy in Manchester loses his dad at a big match. He stays calm, goes to a steward in a bright jacket and shows his wristband. The steward calls the number, and his dad arrives in five minutes.
explain: If a crowd starts pushing, move sideways towards the edge where there is space, keep your arms up in front of your chest, and know where the nearest exit is.
fact: Keep money in a zipped pocket or a closed wallet, because it is harder to lose or steal.
fact: Use a cash machine only with an adult, and cover the keypad so nobody can watch the PIN.
fact: A PIN is a secret number; anyone who sees it and gets the card can take money.
fact: If someone asks for money in the street, stay with your adult and let them decide; it is not your job to decide.
fact: At a big crowd, hold an adult's hand and agree a meeting point before you go in, because it is easy to get lost.
fact: A wristband with a parent's phone number lets staff call your parent if you get lost.
fact: If you get lost at an event, go to the meeting point or ask a steward, a police officer or a staff member in uniform.
fact: When a crowd pushes, move to the side towards space; a crowd squeezing too tightly can crush people.
fact: At a firework display, stand behind the barrier, because fireworks can burn and injure people who are too close.
fact: Know where the nearest exit is at events, because it helps you get out quickly in an emergency.
exercise: A girl in Leeds is going to the shops with a ten-pound note. Where is the best place to keep it? | In a zipped pocket or a closed wallet | In her open hand so she can see it all the time | In the back pocket of her jeans | Tucked into the top of her sock
exercise: Why should money go in a zipped pocket? | It is harder to lose or steal | It keeps the money from getting old and torn | Shops only take money from zipped pockets | Coins lose value when they get cold
exercise: A boy in Texas goes with his mum to a cash machine. What should he help her do? | Cover the keypad while the PIN is typed | Read the PIN out loud to check it | Hold the card up so the screen can see it | Stand far away and wait by the road
exercise: Why do people cover the keypad at a cash machine? | So nobody nearby can watch the secret PIN | So the buttons do not get dusty or sticky | So the machine works faster | So the screen is easier to read in bright sunshine
exercise: A man in the street asks a girl for some money while she is with her aunt. What is right? | Stay with her aunt and let her aunt decide | Give him all her pocket money at once | Walk off with him to find a shop | Argue with him about whether he really needs it or not
exercise: You are going to a big music festival in Ireland with your parents. What should you agree first? | A meeting point in case you get separated | Which songs you want to hear first | Who will carry all the snacks and the bags home | How many photos you will take
exercise: Why should a young child hold an adult's hand in a big crowd? | It is very easy to get separated and lost | Crowds are always angry and dangerous | Adults cannot walk without help | Tickets are only valid for pairs who hold hands at the gate
exercise: What is a wristband with a phone number for at a big event? | So staff can call your parent if you get lost | So you can get free food at the stalls all day | So you can go backstage to meet the band | So the gates know how old you are
exercise: At a football match the crowd starts pushing hard behind you. What should you do? | Move sideways towards the edge where there is space | Push back as hard as you can | Sit down on the ground and wait | Run forward into the thickest part of the crowd to get through
exercise: Why can a crowd that pushes too hard be dangerous? | People can be squeezed so tightly that they are crushed | Crowds always start fights | The noise can make people deaf at once | People in crowds forget where they live and who they came with
exercise: At a Fourth of July firework show in Florida, where should a family stand? | Behind the barrier the organisers set up | As close to the launch spot as they can get | Right under where the fireworks go up, for the best view | Next to the box of unused fireworks
exercise: Why are there barriers at firework displays? | Fireworks can burn and injure people standing too close | So people do not block the view of others | So the fireworks are louder for the crowd | Because fireworks only work in big empty spaces with no people
exercise: When you arrive at a concert hall, what is useful to notice? | Where the nearest exit is | Where the most famous person sits | How many seats are in each row | What colour the stage curtains are
exercise: Why does knowing the nearest exit matter? | It helps you get out quickly in an emergency | It lets you leave early without paying for a ticket | It is where the best food stall in the hall is | It is where the music sounds the loudest and best
exercise: A girl gets lost at a parade in New York. Who is a good person to go to? | A police officer or a staff member in uniform | A stranger who offers her a lift home in his car | Nobody; she should search alone | Any person who says they know her mum
test: A boy in Sydney keeps his birthday money loose in his shorts' back pocket at a busy market. What is the risk? | It can fall out or be taken without him noticing | It will turn into coins | Shops will not accept it | Nothing, back pockets are the safest place for notes and coins
test: Someone behind your dad at a cashpoint is standing very close and looking at the screen. What makes sense? | Cover the keypad and wait for the person to step back | Read the PIN to them so they can help | Leave the card in the machine and walk away | Ask them to type the PIN in instead
test: Your family is at a huge rugby match in Auckland. Before the game, what plan helps most if you get separated? | A meeting point and a wristband with Mum's number | A plan to shout loudly until someone comes to find you | A promise to stay in the car park alone | A plan to walk home by yourself
test: A crowd at a festival gate is squeezing more and more. Which move is safest? | Go sideways towards space | Climb over people towards the gate | Lie down and cover your head | Push forward to get through faster
test: Which is TRUE about firework displays? | Standing behind the barrier keeps you a safe distance away | Fireworks are safe once they leave the ground | Barriers are there only to keep the grass tidy | Children may stand closer than adults
test: A woman asks a boy for money at a bus stop while he is with his grandpa. Whose decision is it? | His grandpa's; the boy stays with him and lets him decide | The boy's, because she asked him directly and not his grandpa | The bus driver's, because it is a bus stop | Nobody's; he must always give money
test: In a dark cinema a fire alarm rings. What knowledge helps most now? | Knowing where the nearest exit is | Knowing which film is showing next | Knowing where the toilets are | Knowing how much the tickets cost
test: Why do many big events give children a wristband to write a phone number on? | So staff can reach a parent quickly if a child is lost | So children can buy things without money | So children can skip the queue | So the event can count how many came
test: A girl lost at a big match remembers the meeting point by the north gate. What should she do? | Go to the meeting point or ask a steward for help | Leave the stadium and look in the car park | Wait in the toilets until the match ends | Follow the first family walking past towards the exit
test: big festival, how do we not lose each other | Hold hands and agree a meeting point | Just shout really loudly if you get lost | Split up so that each of you can see more | Go home on your own if you lose each other
test: crowd's squeezing me bad | Move to the side towards space | Push forward harder to get out | Sit down where you are | Go against the crowd
test: man asked me for a dollar in the street | Stay with your adult and let them decide | Give him all your money so he goes away fast | Go off with him to a shop to buy him food | Run away from your adult to get away from him
test: lost at the concert, can't see dad | Go to the meeting point or ask a steward | Leave and walk home | Ask a random person for a lift | Hide under the stage till it's over
explain: A girl in Manchester kept her money in an open back pocket at a fair; when she went to pay for a ride, it was gone, and she spent the evening watching others.
explain: At a big concert in Australia a boy got pushed into the crowd and lost his parents; because they had agreed to meet at the first-aid tent, he walked there and they found him in ten minutes.
fact: In a tight crowd a person can be squeezed so hard that breathing becomes difficult, which is why you move to the side when a crowd starts to push.
fact: Pickpockets look for open bags and back pockets in busy places, because money there is easy to take.
test: crowd pushing like mad at the gate, cant breathe much | Move sideways to the edge | Push back as hard as you can | Sit down where you are | Climb over people to the front
test: lost my dad at the match, phone's dead | Go to the meeting point or ask a steward | Leave and walk home | Go with anyone who says they know him and will take you to him | Hide and wait till it's over
test: man in the street asks me for money what do i do | Stay with your adult and let them decide | Give him your wallet so he can choose | Go with him to a cash machine | Run off and leave your adult
test: did u hear at the concert ppl got "squashed like sardines, one lady couldn't breathe". real lesson? | If a crowd pushes, move to the side towards space | Push harder to get to the front, where there is more air | Stand still right in the middle | Lie down so you can't fall over
test: my aunt says a pickpocket took "500 quid" from a kid's back pocket at the market. what's prob exaggerated | The amount; keep money in a zipped pocket | That pickpockets exist at all | That markets get busy | That back pockets are easy to reach
test: kids say the man asking for money outside the shop is "a fake, he's got a mansion". spread that? | No, it's only a rumour; let your adult decide | Yes, warn everyone he's a fake | Yes, and shout it at him | Yes, because it's funny
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the correct conduct of a person when a crowd begins to press? | The person shall move to the side, towards open space | The person shall push forward with all available strength | The person shall remain motionless at the centre | The person shall sit down upon the ground
exam: Identify the principal purpose of agreeing a meeting point prior to entering a large crowd. | A place to reunite should the party become separated | It secures a superior view of the event | It reduces the cost of admission | It ensures the party departs before the crowd and avoids the queues
exam: In respect of cash machines, select the response consistent with the established facts. | A child shall use one only with an adult, covering the keypad | A child may use one alone provided the PIN is spoken quietly | The keypad need not be covered in daylight | The PIN may be written upon the card for safety
exam: State the reason for which spectators at a firework display remain behind the barrier. | Fireworks may injure persons standing too close | The barrier marks the area reserved for paying guests | The fireworks cannot be seen from beyond it | Smoke is prohibited from crossing the barrier
